WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 244: Henry Clay: The Great Pacificator, the American System, and the Compromise of 1850

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:06.080
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:18.800
[Rebecca] Today, our journey through the top 50 famous non president Americans follows the charismatic orator and parliamentary giant who repeatedly saved the American Republic from fracturing into civil war.

3
00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:24.240
[Rebecca] Henry Clay, the Great Pacificator.

4
00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:37.920
[George] Born in 1777 in the swampy pine lowlands of Hanover County, Virginia, an area known as the Slashes, Henry Clay was the seventh child of a Baptist minister who died when Henry was just four years old.

5
00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:49.600
[George] Barefoot and riding a plough horse with a rope bridle, young Henry hauled bags of cornmeal to the local gristmill, earning the affectionate frontier nickname, the mill boy of the slashes.

6
00:00:49.600 --> 00:01:02.160
[George] Despite little formal schooling, young Henry possessed an extraordinary natural gift for language, learning law as a copyist in Richmond under the legendary George Wyth, who recognized his brilliant intellect.

7
00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:11.840
[George] In 1797, at age 20, Clay crossed the Cumberland Gap into the booming frontier Commonwealth of Kentucky, settling in Lexington.

8
00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:22.880
[George] Possessed of magnetic personal charm, dazzling wit, and a voice of musical resonance that could command courtroom crowds, Clay became the most sought-after defense lawyer in the West.

9
00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:36.160
[George] He purchased a grand 600 acre estate near Lexington, which he named Ashland, breeding thoroughbred racehorses and Durham cattle, becoming the undisputed political champion of the rising American frontier.

10
00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:48.640
[George] In Lexington, Clay married Lucretia Hart, daughter of a wealthy merchant who managed the Ashland estate with legendary competence, overseeing thoroughbred breeding and agricultural experiments.

11
00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:58.960
[George] Clay's mother Elizabeth Hudson had buried her husband during the Revolutionary War, teaching young Henry that perseverance through adversity was the mark of true moral character.

12
00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:07.160
[George] Elected to the United States House of Representatives in 1811, 34-year-old Henry Clay achieved an unprecedented feat.

13
00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:11.160
[George] He was elected Speaker of the House on his very first day in office.

14
00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:16.840
[George] Up to that time, the Speaker had been a passive moderator who merely kept order in debates.

15
00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:28.200
[George] Clay transformed the Speakership into the most powerful legislative office in America, using committee appointments, parliamentary rulings and personal charisma to drive national policy.

16
00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:43.240
[George] Clay was the fiery leader of the Warhawks, a new generation of young Western and Southern Congressmen who refused to tolerate British naval impressment of American sailors and trade restrictions, rallying Congress to declare the war of 1812.

17
00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:53.480
[George] In 1814, Clay was appointed by President Madison as a peace commissioner to Ghent in Belgium, joining John Quincy Adams to negotiate the Treaty of Ghent.

18
00:02:53.480 --> 00:03:08.520
[George] In the conference rooms, Clay's unyielding diplomatic resolve, poker playing instincts, and refusal to surrender American fishing rights or Mississippi Navigation forced British diplomats to agree to peace without American territorial concessions.

19
00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:11.240
[George] Clay was renowned for his forensic wit.

20
00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:21.560
[George] When an opponent sneered that Clay's voice was too theatrical, Clay smiled and replied, I speak to the heart, sir, because the heart cannot be deceived by sophistry.

21
00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:33.560
[George] As a young lawyer in Kentucky, Clay defended Aaron Burr against federal charges with such brilliance that Burr was acquitted, though Clay later severed all ties upon learning of Burr's conspiracies.

22
00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:51.040
[George] In 1819, the young American Republic confronted its first existential sectional crisis over slavery when the territory of Missouri applied for admission to the Union as a slave state, threatening to upset the delicate balance of eleven free states and eleven slave states in the Senate.

23
00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:54.000
[George] Bitter debate erupted in Congress.

24
00:03:54.000 --> 00:04:01.040
[George] Northern representatives sought to ban slavery in Missouri, while Southern leaders threatened disunion and armed resistance.

25
00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:09.200
[George] Thomas Jefferson wrote from Monticello that the crisis awakened him like a fire bell in the night, and sounded like the death knell of the Union.

26
00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:12.880
[George] Into this terrifying breach stepped Speaker Henry Clay.

27
00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:23.680
[George] Working tirelessly behind the scenes for weeks, cajoling, persuading, and uniting moderate congressmen, Clay crafted the historic Missouri Compromise of 1820.

28
00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:37.040
[George] Under the plan, Maine was admitted as a free state, Missouri as a slave state, and slavery was permanently banned in all remaining Louisiana purchased territories north of the 36 degrees 30 parallel.

29
00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:45.040
[George] Clay's compromise defused the immediate threat of national disintegration, earning him the immortal title of the Great Pacificator.

30
00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:57.360
[George] The Missouri Compromise line at 36 degrees 30 minutes remained the sacred boundary of American territorial peace for 34 years until repealed by the Kansas-Nebraska Act in 1854.

31
00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:09.360
[George] The Missouri Compromise was passed in the House by a razor-thin margin of 90 to 87, demonstrating Clay's superhuman mastery of legislative persuasion under extreme political pressure.

32
00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:16.320
[George] Clay was not merely a legislative peacemaker, he was the visionary economic architect of modern America.

33
00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:26.160
[George] Recognizing that the United States was vulnerable to foreign economic dominance, Clay formulated a comprehensive domestic policy known as the American system.

34
00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:39.160
[George] Clay envisioned an economically self-sufficient continental superpower binding all sections of the country in mutual prosperity, the industrialized North, the agricultural West, and the plantation South.

35
00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:43.560
[George] The American system rested upon three mutually reinforcing pillars.

36
00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:50.120
[George] First, high protective tariffs on foreign manufactured goods to nurture infant American industries.

37
00:05:50.120 --> 00:06:02.040
[George] Second, a robust national bank to provide a stable national currency and credit system, and third, federal financing of internal improvements, building nationwide networks of roads.

38
00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:10.280
[George] Canals, bridges, and dredged harbors to link Western farmers with eastern markets, personified by the historic National Road.

39
00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:21.160
[George] Abraham Lincoln later idolized Clay, calling him my beau ideal of a statesman, and adopting Clay's American system as the economic blueprint for the Republican Party.

40
00:06:21.160 --> 00:06:33.880
[George] Clay's American system directly linked national defense to commercial prosperity, arguing that a nation that depends on foreign countries for iron, cloth, and coal is always vulnerable to military subjugation.

41
00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:46.120
[George] Clay's American system championed the construction of the Erie Canal and the Chesapeake and Ohio Canal, demonstrating how federal and state infrastructure investments could unlock continental commerce.

42
00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:58.600
[George] In the chaotic four-way presidential election of 1824, no candidate won an electoral majority, throwing the election into the House of Representatives between Andrew Jackson and John Quincy Adams.

43
00:06:58.600 --> 00:07:01.720
[George] As Speaker, Clay held the deciding vote.

44
00:07:01.720 --> 00:07:12.920
[George] Viewing Jackson as a dangerous, ignorant military chieftain whose violent temper was unfit for the presidency, Clay cast his support for Adams, securing Adams's election.

45
00:07:12.920 --> 00:07:27.120
[George] When Adams subsequently appointed Clay as Secretary of State, Jackson's furious supporters cried foul, branding the arrangement, the corrupt bargain, a toxic political slander that haunted Clay for the remainder of his life.

46
00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:35.040
[George] For the next three decades, Clay served in the United States Senate, forming with Daniel Webster of Massachusetts and John C.

47
00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:45.520
[George] Calhoun of South Carolina, the legendary Great Triumvirate, the three parliamentary giants who dominated American politics during the Golden Age of Senate debate.

48
00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:50.000
[George] Clay founded the Whig Party to oppose Jacksonian executive tyranny.

49
00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:56.960
[George] Running for president three times and uttering his famous noble creed, I would rather be right than be president.

50
00:07:56.960 --> 00:08:00.000
[George] Clay's private life was shadowed by heartbreak.

51
00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:05.760
[George] All six of his beloved daughters died young, yet he returned to the Senate after each tragedy.

52
00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:08.720
[George] Burying his grief in service to the Republic.

53
00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:19.840
[George] In 1832, Clay challenged Andrew Jackson on the recharter of the Second Bank of the United States, sparking the bank war that defined the political divide of the Jacksonian era.

54
00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:27.040
[George] In 1832, the Union faced a direct threat of civil war when South Carolina, led by John C.

55
00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:42.240
[George] Calhoun, passed the ordinance of nullification, declaring the federal protective tariffs of 1828 and 1832 null and void within its borders, threatening to secede if the federal government attempted to collect duties by force.

56
00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:55.680
[George] President Andrew Jackson reacted with furious military resolve, issuing a proclamation condemning nullification as treason and preparing to send federal warships and troops to Charleston to hang Calhoun and enforce the law.

57
00:08:55.680 --> 00:09:03.160
[George] With South Carolina mobilizing volunteer militias and civil war days away, Clay stepped forward once again.

58
00:09:03.160 --> 00:09:20.200
[George] Overcoming his bitter personal hatred for Jackson and ideological disputes with Calhoun, Clay drafted the Compromise Tariff of 1833, which gradually reduced tariff rates over a ten-year period while allowing South Carolina to rescind its nullification ordinance with dignity.

59
00:09:20.200 --> 00:09:29.000
[George] Through sheer legislative genius, Clay averted armed conflict, preserving the Union without shedding a single drop of American blood.

60
00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:40.600
[George] During the nullification crisis, Clay worked around the clock in Washington boarding houses, reconciling the conflicting tariff calculations of New England industrialists and southern planters.

61
00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:52.520
[George] The compromise tariff of 1833 was crafted in secret meetings between Clay and Calhoun, proving that even mortal political enemies could find common ground for the survival of the Republic.

62
00:09:52.520 --> 00:10:01.560
[George] In January 1850, 72-year-old Henry Clay returned to a divided Senate for his final, greatest legislative crusade.

63
00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:11.560
[George] The discovery of gold in California and the acquisition of vast Southwestern territories from Mexico had reopened the wound of slavery with catastrophic intensity.

64
00:10:11.560 --> 00:10:16.760
[George] Southern senators threatened open secession if California was admitted as a free state.

65
00:10:16.760 --> 00:10:32.120
[George] Coughing with tuberculosis and leaning upon a walking stick, Clay rose in the Senate chamber to deliver a monumental two day speech, holding aloft a splinter of George Washington's coffin as he pleaded for union, mutual concession, and fraternal love.

66
00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:48.240
[George] He proposed an omnibus compromise, California admitted as a free state, popular sovereignty in Utah and New Mexico, the abolition of the slave trade in Washington, D.C., and a new, stricter federal fugitive slave act, aided by Stephen A.

67
00:10:48.800 --> 00:11:03.280
[George] Douglas and Daniel Webster, the Compromise of 1850 passed, postponing the Civil War for 11 crucial years, allowing the northern states to build the industrial capacity and railway infrastructure that ultimately saved the Union.

68
00:11:03.280 --> 00:11:20.800
[George] When Henry Clay died in Washington on June 29, 1852, he became the first person to lie in state in the United States Capitol Rotunda, remembered by a grateful nation as the great compromiser whose love of the American Union surpassed all party, section, and self.

69
00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:33.280
[George] Clay's final Senate speeches in 1850 were delivered while suffering from burning lung fever, coughing blood into his handkerchief, yet his resonant voice never lost its musical, persuasive power.

70
00:11:33.280 --> 00:11:44.720
[George] Today, the Ashland estate in Lexington stands as a national historic landmark, where the towering ginkgo trees planted by Henry Clay still bloom in honor of the great Pacificator.

71
00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:48.480
[Rebecca] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

72
00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:54.800
[Rebecca] Next week, our top 50 non president American series continues with episode 245.

73
00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:12.320
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

